    </seriesStmt><sourceDesc><p>Born digital.</p></sourceDesc></fileDesc><revisionDesc><change who="http://syriaca.org/documentation/editors.xml#autogenerated" when="2026-07-28-05:00">CREATED: This bibl record was autogenerated from a Zotero record.</change><change who="https://www.zotero.org/cbss_admin" when="2022-11-18T14:34:41Z">Record created in Zotero.</change></revisionDesc></teiHeader><text><body><biblStruct type="journalArticle"><analytic><author><forename>Kristine J.</forename><surname>Ruffatto</surname></author><title level="a">The Concept of Rest in the Greek &lt;i&gt;Acts of Thomas&lt;/i&gt;</title><idno type="URI">http://syriaca.org/cbss/PK9LJGJB</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/a_comprehensive_bibliography_on_syriac_studies/items/PK9LJGJB</idno><idno type="URI">https://www.zotero.org/groups/4861694/items/PK9LJGJB</idno></analytic><monogr><title level="j">Orientalia Christiana Periodica</title><imprint><date>2009</date></imprint><biblScope unit="vol">75:1</biblScope><biblScope unit="pp">133-155</biblScope></monogr><note type="abstract">Rest is an important soteriological and christological concept in the third century apocryphal &lt;i&gt;Acts of Thomas&lt;/i&gt;. The forty-five references to rest (ἀνάπαυσις and its cognates) mark rest as a significant theme of the work. This article provides an analysis of the use of ἀνάπαυσις in the Greek &lt;i&gt;Acts of Thomas&lt;/i&gt;. Rest is a rich and multifaceted motif that expresses both present experience and future hope. It is an attribute of Christ but also a benefit of being in union with Christ. Rest, achieved through rigorous “holiness” (abstinence, especially from sex), is synonymous with salvation. The &lt;i&gt;Acts of Thomas&lt;/i&gt;’ understanding of rest is rooted in Jewish rest tradition, which the author/compiler has christianized and enriched with an ascetical emphasis that is characteristic of Syriac spirituality.</note></biblStruct><bibl type="formatted" subtype="bibliography" resp="https://www.zotero.org/styles/chicago-note-bibliography-17th-edition">
  Ruffatto, Kristine J. “The Concept of Rest in the Greek <title level="m">Acts of Thomas</title>.” <title level="m">Orientalia Christiana Periodica</title> 75:1 (2009): 133–55.
